# FRICTION.log

Running log of everything that costs time while building against Uniswap. One entry per incident,
appended when it happens — this file is the raw material `FEEDBACK.md` is edited down from.

Format:

    YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM  [area]  what I expected -> what actually happened  (cost: Xm)

`area` is the surface, e.g. v4-core, v4-periphery, v3-periphery, quoter, hooks, docs, tooling.
Record the expectation before the outcome; "I assumed X was true because the docs say Y" is the
part that makes the feedback actionable and the part that is impossible to reconstruct later.

---

2026-09-04 09:00  [meta]  log started at D1, per plan.

2026-09-04 18:20  [v3-periphery/v4-core]  pragma pinning, avoided rather than hit.
    Expected to import Uniswap sources directly and compile them alongside the callback.
    Could not: v3 implementations are `=0.7.6`, v4's PoolManager.sol/PositionManager.sol are
    exactly `0.8.26`, and this project follows Midnight at 0.8.34. Wrote test/interfaces/
    IUniswapMinimal.sol by hand instead and bound the already-deployed Base contracts.
    Worth stating precisely in FEEDBACK.md: the cost here was NOT a multi-profile build, because
    fork-testing against a chain where everything is deployed sidesteps it entirely. The cost was
    having to know that workaround exists. It is not in any Uniswap integration doc I found.
    (cost: ~25m, mostly writing interfaces by hand)

2026-09-04 18:35  [docs]  no canonical machine-readable deployment list found for Base.
    Expected an official addresses page/JSON to resolve v3 factory, NonfungiblePositionManager,
    v4 PoolManager and PositionManager on Base. Ended up cross-validating on-chain instead:
    NPM.factory() == v3 factory, PositionManager.poolManager() == PoolManager,
    factory.feeAmountTickSpacing(500) == 10. That worked and is arguably better practice, but it
    is a verification step every integrator repeats from scratch.
    (cost: ~15m)

2026-09-05 --:--  [v4-core/v4-periphery]  LiquidityAmounts is not where you need it.
    Expected to compute token amounts from a position's liquidity using LiquidityAmounts, which is
    what every guide and every v3 integration uses. It is in v4-PERIPHERY, not v4-core. Taking a
    periphery dependency to get one pure function that touches no protocol state was not worth it,
    so composed it by hand from SqrtPriceMath.getAmount0Delta/getAmount1Delta plus the three
    range cases. That is ~15 lines that exist in Uniswap's own repos twice already.
    The split is defensible for contracts; it is odd for pure math with no protocol dependency.
    (cost: ~20m, mostly deciding whether to take the dependency)

2026-09-05 --:--  [v4-core]  v3->v4 constant rename with no migration note found.
    Expected v3's MIN_SQRT_RATIO/MAX_SQRT_RATIO in v4's TickMath. They are MIN_SQRT_PRICE and
    MAX_SQRT_PRICE. Values and tick range are identical, so v4's TickMath is safe to point at a v3
    pool -- which is exactly what this project does, since v3's own libraries are pinned to =0.7.6
    and cannot be compiled alongside it. Had to diff the constants by hand to establish that it
    was a rename and not a semantic change. A one-line note in either repo would have covered it.
    (cost: ~10m)

2026-09-05 --:--  [tooling]  adding v4-core silently repointed an existing remapping.
    Expected `forge remappings` to gain v4-core entries. It also moved `ds-test/` from
    lib/midnight/lib/morpho-blue/lib/forge-std/... to lib/v4-core/lib/forge-std/... Nothing broke,
    but a transitive dependency of a newly added submodule quietly rebinding a remapping that an
    existing dependency relies on is a real hazard in a multi-submodule project.
    Also worth noting the shape inconsistency it creates: forge generates `v4-core/=lib/v4-core/src/`
    but `midnight/=lib/midnight/`, so imports read `v4-core/libraries/X.sol` next to
    `midnight/src/libraries/X.sol`. Cost one failed build to notice.
    (cost: ~10m)

2026-09-05 --:--  [v3-periphery]  positions() returns twelve values.
    Expected to read a position's ticks and liquidity. INonfungiblePositionManager.positions
    returns a 12-tuple with no struct, so every call site is a line of anonymous commas where a
    miscount compiles fine and reads the wrong field. Wrapped it in a local struct immediately.
    Not a blocker, but it is the kind of thing that makes an integration bug silent.
    (cost: ~5m)
